PASS level_intents: all 120 levels have a specific intent
PASS no_filler_levels: no trivial filler levels after the tutorial
FAIL level_design: level-design review 7.4/10 (bar 8), 1 level(s) below 6/10: A well-built, honest 120-level campaign. Almost every level has a named aha, a written trap and a proof, and the cities follow a clean teach, practise, combine, twist, boss, breather, mastery rhythm. It is fair on the data: trap rate is 0 wherever measured, and nothing looks like guessing. Three weaknesses keep it below the best of the genre. (1) Aha repetition: "one long snake loses to two short lines" is the answer to roughly half the levels. "Skip the silent or 0-rider stop" recurs in L8, 17, 32, 43, 89, 90, 94, 107. "The heavy pair shares a line" recurs in L9, 20, 56, 70, 106, 112, 120. "Keep a line end free for the late station" recurs in L4, 6, 8, 11, 12, 16, 18, 22, 44, 47. Monument Valley and Flow Free keep surprising where this set starts to pattern-match. (2) Late combines stack 3-4 rules (L113, L118, L107). Several are labelled Practise or Combine but are really mini-bosses. (3) Some levels turn on number-reading rather than seeing the board (hop and seat arithmetic, 27 vs 36 seats). Calm players get a spreadsheet more than a picture, which clashes with the 'gentlest metro builder' promise. Difficulty targets are met: L1-3 are 87-97% casual, the last third averages about 55% casual, and there are no easy streaks. Opening levels 1-3 are soft on stakes because overcrowding is off. Unverified '?' trap rates on about 20 levels (L70, 77, 78, 106 and others) should be swept before launch. Curve: Pacing is disciplined. Each city introduces one rule alone (lines/transfers, tunnels, carriages, hills, ferries, snow, interchanges/markets, loops/arrows, waves, heritage), then combines, then twists, with a boss near the middle or end and a gentle mastery send-off. Breathers (L7, 19, 31, 43, 55, 67, 79, 91, 103, 115) land after hard levels and are mostly easier than their neighbours. Casual win rates fall steadily from about 90% to about 40-55% by city 10. That meets the targets but means the final stretch (L97-120) is a long run of 47-62% levels with only L103 and L108 as relief. Weaknesses: (1) The first city runs 12 levels with 4 ideas at 81-97% casual and overcrowding off in L1-3, so stakes arrive late. (2) Mechanics are introduced strictly alone, but from city 5 onward combines pile up 3-4 rules at once (L113, 118, 107, 120). Add one more 'one new rule, simple board' level before each such jump. (3) Aha repetition (snake vs short lines, silent-stop skip, heavy-pair-share-a-line, late-station free end) means the middle 60 levels feel like the same question with new costumes. Replace or merge the weakest repeats (L94, 106, 112, 56) with levels that use a genuinely new decision. (4) Numeric reasoning (hops, seats) dominates and the visual 'see the shape' aha is rare. Add more levels like L7/L45/L76/L93 where the board itself reveals the answer. (5) Boss and mastery levels with '?' trap rates need the full fairness sweep before launch.
REDESIGN THESE LEVELS (keep the ones not listed):
L94 (5/10): Repeats L89/L90 skip-the-silent-stop and L85 loop beats open line. → Replace with a level where two quiet stops compete for the one omission.
L7 (6/10): Win is trivial and the only puzzle is a Hamiltonian-path drawing; the river commas in the middle are clutter. → Remove the decorative river or make it a real feature, and move the dead-end puzzle to a 3x3 with two plausible dead ends.
L17 (6/10): Decoy 0-rider station is an early-reveal gimmick; board is a plain 3+3 grid. → Give E small real demand and make D or F the larger, so the tunnel choice is a weighed trade.
L56 (6/10): Heavy-pair-gets-express repeat of L9/L20 with a new costume; casual 67%. → Replace with a level where the ferry's cap on a loop-less line, not the pair, is the thought.
L106 (6/10): Third rerun of heavy-pair express (L9, L20); trap rate '?'. → Replace with a level where the express hurts, e.g. the hill line that swallows the loop.
L112 (6/10): Same shared-line aha as L9/L12; casual 50% in a combine. → Add a third hall and force the shared stop to be a choice.
L113 (6/10): Tunnel, loop, wave and heritage all at once under a 'Practise' label. → Remove the wave and relabel.
L118 (6/10): Casual 47% and four rules in a combine. → Remove heritage or the ferry.
L1 (7/10): Thin aha; with overcrowding off nothing is at stake, and 3 stars are nearly given. → Make A's riders visibly need C so the wk2 extension is a discovery rather than an instruction; add a tiny 4th stop's worth of choice.
L2 (7/10): Fine but the 'single line falls short' is shown only by numbers. → Show the stranded D riders visibly queueing in the wk1 forecast so the need for a second line is seen, not read.
L8 (7/10): Skipping a 0-rider stop is the first of many 'ignore the silent stop' ahas; B's logic relies on reading a station nobody uses. → Keep the free-end idea but give B real riders so the skip is a trade-off, not a freebie.
L11 (7/10): Mostly a recombination of L4/L6; trap rate '?' and goal margin is thin (193 of 219). → Finish the fairness sweep; add one decision that is new, such as the card changing which plan is correct.
L12 (7/10): Strong idea (must-share stop) but also a near-rerun of L6/L11 (snake vs short lines plus late stop). → Drop the 'snake loses' framing and make the shared stop the only lesson.
L14 (7/10): Close to L13 (choose where to cross); aha is mostly a restatement. → Add a late station or unequal demand so the corner crossing is not the only reading.
L16 (7/10): Card reading depends on noticing F's demand; the tunnel card is a tempting dud, which is borderline a guess. → Let the card preview show the stranded ring so the choice is informed, not discoverable only in failure.
L19 (7/10): 93% casual right after a 77% boss is correct, but the 3-hop shortcut aha is mild. → Fine; trim the 7 track surplus so the shortcut tempts.
L25 (7/10): Aha is 'add the carriage'; the trap is just not doing the only new thing. → Offer two lines and a limited carriage so placement is the thought.
L31 (7/10): Almost no decision; a pretty shape with no insight. → Make the single-line star require a clean path choice (which side of the kite to skip).
L32 (7/10): Skilled 100% and a 0-rider decoy; the aha is only about the star. → Add a tempting use for the fifth track so holding back costs something real.
L36 (7/10): Single line and one card; the carriage answer is obvious from L25. → Add a second, tempting card use (e.g. track spend) so the pick is read from the forecast.
L37 (7/10): Near copy of L25 structurally; hop count is the whole game. → Show the hop count on the hill as a visible slowdown on the train.
L39 (7/10): Hard to read; numbers (9 hops vs 7) are not obvious from the board. → Label each route's hops in the preview and shrink the ring so the long flat road is visibly long.
L41 (7/10): Repeats L39 and L38 ideas; the C stop is noise. → Merge with L39 or replace with a ridge level where the climb IS worth it.
L43 (7/10): Second path-drawing breather like L7; two 0-rider stops. → Vary the texture (e.g. pick which of two temples to include).
L47 (7/10): Many rules (late stop, two climbs, carriage you may not need); skilled only 86% and casual 66%, so it leans on hidden hop arithmetic. → Remove the steep-road decoy or the carriage so one decision is visible from week 1.

level-design score 7.4/10 — A well-built, honest 120-level campaign. Almost every level has a named aha, a written trap and a proof, and the cities follow a clean teach, practise, combine, twist, boss, breather, mastery rhythm. It is fair on the data: trap rate is 0 wherever measured, and nothing looks like guessing. Three weaknesses keep it below the best of the genre. (1) Aha repetition: "one long snake loses to two short lines" is the answer to roughly half the levels. "Skip the silent or 0-rider stop" recurs in L8, 17, 32, 43, 89, 90, 94, 107. "The heavy pair shares a line" recurs in L9, 20, 56, 70, 106, 112, 120. "Keep a line end free for the late station" recurs in L4, 6, 8, 11, 12, 16, 18, 22, 44, 47. Monument Valley and Flow Free keep surprising where this set starts to pattern-match. (2) Late combines stack 3-4 rules (L113, L118, L107). Several are labelled Practise or Combine but are really mini-bosses. (3) Some levels turn on number-reading rather than seeing the board (hop and seat arithmetic, 27 vs 36 seats). Calm players get a spreadsheet more than a picture, which clashes with the 'gentlest metro builder' promise. Difficulty targets are met: L1-3 are 87-97% casual, the last third averages about 55% casual, and there are no easy streaks. Opening levels 1-3 are soft on stakes because overcrowding is off. Unverified '?' trap rates on about 20 levels (L70, 77, 78, 106 and others) should be swept before launch.
curve: Pacing is disciplined. Each city introduces one rule alone (lines/transfers, tunnels, carriages, hills, ferries, snow, interchanges/markets, loops/arrows, waves, heritage), then combines, then twists, with a boss near the middle or end and a gentle mastery send-off. Breathers (L7, 19, 31, 43, 55, 67, 79, 91, 103, 115) land after hard levels and are mostly easier than their neighbours. Casual win rates fall steadily from about 90% to about 40-55% by city 10. That meets the targets but means the final stretch (L97-120) is a long run of 47-62% levels with only L103 and L108 as relief. Weaknesses: (1) The first city runs 12 levels with 4 ideas at 81-97% casual and overcrowding off in L1-3, so stakes arrive late. (2) Mechanics are introduced strictly alone, but from city 5 onward combines pile up 3-4 rules at once (L113, 118, 107, 120). Add one more 'one new rule, simple board' level before each such jump. (3) Aha repetition (snake vs short lines, silent-stop skip, heavy-pair-share-a-line, late-station free end) means the middle 60 levels feel like the same question with new costumes. Replace or merge the weakest repeats (L94, 106, 112, 56) with levels that use a genuinely new decision. (4) Numeric reasoning (hops, seats) dominates and the visual 'see the shape' aha is rare. Add more levels like L7/L45/L76/L93 where the board itself reveals the answer. (5) Boss and mastery levels with '?' trap rates need the full fairness sweep before launch.
